00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.670
why don't you tell us a
little bit about yourself.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.000
What do you do at Google?

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.000
MYLES BORINS: Yeah, so as was
mentioned, my name is Myles.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.290
My other name is Borins.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.870
Together, it's Myles Borins.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:09.590
And I'm a developer advocate
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.230
I focus primarily on Node.js.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:17.037
But I also dabble in Compute and
Kubernetes and Cloud Functions

00:04:17.037 --> 00:04:18.620
and pretty much
anywhere where you can

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.170
run Node or do Nodey things.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.850
I'm on the Technical
Steering Committee

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.390
of Node.js Foundation,
where I was recently

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.090
elected the Technical
Steering Committee director.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.680
So I also sit on the board of
directors of the Foundation.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.970
I'm one of the primary
release managers running

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.170
most of our LTS programs.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.870
So most of the version
4 and version 6 releases

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.630
and some of the version 8
releases are coming from me.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.352
And lately, most of my work
has actually been around V8

00:04:45.352 --> 00:04:47.060
and making sure that
the Node project can

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.640
have the most modern
versions of V8 as possible.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.390
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.670
So actually, why don't we
start with some history.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:54.830
Most people probably
know what Node is.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.330
But maybe, some people don't.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.160
So why don't you tell us a
little bit about the language,

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.004
where it came from.

00:04:59.004 --> 00:05:00.170
What's that background like?

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.820
MYLES BORINS: Yeah, totally.

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.050
I'll give a super abridged
version of the keynote

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.380
that I gave at Node Interactive
recently, Google was there.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.840
We had about five different
talks and three keynotes.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.540
And one of mine was one
of the closing keynotes.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:14.844
And I called it Node wave 6.

00:05:14.844 --> 00:05:16.510
And it was just about
how there has been

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.400
many different ways of Node.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.560
Node was originally created
in 2009 by Ryan Dahl

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.127
and presented a JSComp VU.

00:05:24.127 --> 00:05:25.960
That would be what we
would consider wave 1.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.830
And Ryan was the BDFL, which is
short for Benevolent Dictator

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.980
for Life, of the project
or about, like, I think,

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.040
two or three years.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.770
And then the project
was taken over

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.900
by Isaac Schluter,
who ran the project

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.190
for a similar amount of time.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.640
And then T.J. Fontaine--
not T.J. Holowaychuk--

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.210
T.J. Fontaine took over for
what I would dub Node wave 3.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:50.410
The project was forked
in 2015 into iOjs,

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.030
which was Node wave 4.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.900
And it was put into a foundation
later that year, which was,

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:57.860
theoretically, Node wave 5.

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.360
And that was the
release of Node.js 4.0.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.190
Since then, Node has
been in a foundation

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.180
for the last two years.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.190
We're up to Node.js 8 right now.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.320
Node.js 9 is coming out
at the end of this month.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.460
And yeah, we've been
doing a lot of discussion

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.100
about Node wave 6,
because we're starting

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.470
to feel a little bit
of the growth pain now

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:20.050
and trying to re-imagine
some of our governance

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.007
and technical direction, to
allow us to continue to scale.

00:06:24.007 --> 00:06:25.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I
think it's pretty cool

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.750
that you were
mentioning at least

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.020
two different organizations.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.330
You mentioned the Technical
Steering Committee.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.400
And you also mentioned
the Foundation.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.104
What are those?

00:06:34.104 --> 00:06:36.020
Let's start with the
Foundation, for instance.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.610
What is the Node Foundation?

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.550
MYLES BORINS: Yes, so
the Node.js Foundation

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:41.350
is a nonprofit.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.990
I believe it's a 501(c)(3)?

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:46.330
That's the thing
that non-profits are?

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:46.630
MARK MANDEL: Sounds about right.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.796
MYLES BORINS: Maybe I got
one of those digits wrong.

00:06:48.796 --> 00:06:51.450
But the Foundation has a number
of different member-partners.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.200
And each of the
member-partners donate money,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.533
based on their membership level.

00:06:54.533 --> 00:06:56.500
There's silver,
gold, and platinum.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.320
Google recently came in
as a platinum sponsor.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.240
And that actually brought one
of our colleagues, Sarah Novotny

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.780
to the board of directors,
which is really exciting.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.320
The board of directors is
made up of representations.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.000
One person for each
member from the platinum

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.580
level of sponsorship and
then a certain number

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.530
of members from both
gold and silver,

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.030
depending on how
many sponsors there

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.980
are in each of those levels.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.965
The board of directors
right now is--

00:07:19.965 --> 00:07:20.590
I want to say--

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:21.760
11 people.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.790
And those 11 individuals
are essentially

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.035
responsible for the fiduciary
duty of the foundation.

00:07:27.035 --> 00:07:29.012
So The Foundation
has all this money.

00:07:29.012 --> 00:07:30.220
What do we use the money for?

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.110
How does it get handled?

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.380
The Foundation itself owns
the codebase of Node.js

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.420
and has lawyers on hand.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.390
So like, if someone
decides to sue us

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.820
because they think that
we're causing problems

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.730
with licenses and stuff, the
Foundation would protect us.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.525
That actually is
one thing that's

00:07:47.525 --> 00:07:49.900
really great about foundations
in general for open source

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.960
software, is it protects
all of the maintainers

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.044
and contributors.

00:07:54.044 --> 00:07:55.960
So if you're a contributor
and you contributed

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:59.500
code that, maybe, somehow down
the line, had some GPL in it

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.300
and you didn't
know, the Foundation

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.730
would be there to
support and help you.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.370
The Technical Steering Committee
and the board of directors

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:08.560
kind of report to each other.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.744
Although, like in a
classic org structure,

00:08:10.744 --> 00:08:12.910
you would maybe see the
board of directors as higher

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.030
than the TSC.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:18.610
I, as the TSC director, am kind
of the emissary between both

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.680
of those organizations.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.500
One of the interesting things
about our technical governance

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.270
though is that the
board of directors

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.550
has no technical oversight
over the project,

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:28.990
nor does the Foundation.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:31.180
The project's
technical governance

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:34.210
is completely overseen by the
Technical Steering Committee

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:35.919
and the volunteers on it.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:37.539
So the board can't
show up and be

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:42.309
like, hey, we want you to bake
GRPC into the binaries of Node.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:43.419
That wouldn't fly.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:47.920
But members of the Google team
who are represented on the TSC

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.640
have been working really hard
to get HTTP2 natively supported,

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.560
not behind a flag,
in Node 8, which

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.500
will allow us to have
non-binary versions of GRPC.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.290
So you can have influence
and corporations

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.360
can drive influence,
but not through money.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:05.210
It's through participation,
action, and engagement.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:08.470
MARK MANDEL: So what
exactly does the TSC do?

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.200
MYLES BORINS: So that's a
little bit of a loaded question,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.033
because we're in the
process of figuring out

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:14.510
what the TSC should do.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.310
What the TSC has
historically done has been--

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.530
responsible for the overall
technical governance

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.610
and direction of the project.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.270
So this has to do with
high-level initiatives

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:26.890
and high-level vision.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:30.070
So we've been doing,
since April, for example,

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:32.500
plans for having more
modern versions of V8

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.270
in the 8.x LTS release.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.100
And that's involved ongoing
work from various TSC members.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.880
At the moment, whenever
there is certain levels

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.450
of technical disagreements,
those get floated to the TSC

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:49.180
to either have the
Technical Steering

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.860
Committee itself reach consensus
over or vote, if necessary.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.570
I think one thing
that's worth mentioning

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.090
is the technical
project of Node itself

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:01.870
is governed in a model
called "consensus seeking."

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:05.110
So essentially, any
person on the planet

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.640
can send a pole request to Node.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.640
And if a committer
has signed off on it

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.940
and it has been there for more
than 48 hours without anyone

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.870
rejecting it, we consider
that seeking consensus.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.740
And it can just land on Master.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.230
We have a number of
processes in place,

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:23.710
from testing to CI
to smoke testing

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.550
to the waterfall of
our release process,

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:29.050
that offer many different lines
of where we can catch things

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:30.880
if they end up
being problematic,

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.380
if they land to quickly.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.070
But generally, we've been
trying to empower our committers

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.400
as much as possible.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:38.890
And we try to, as
much as possible,

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:41.741
not have things go to a vote
and, even further, not go

00:10:41.741 --> 00:10:43.240
to the Technical
Steering Committee.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.490
So we had a Technical Steering
Committee meeting this morning,

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:47.050
for example.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:50.740
And we spent the first
1/3 of it talking

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.250
about the new version of
V8 that recently turned off

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.380
escape analysis,
because of a bug, which

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.997
makes async/await slower.

00:10:57.997 --> 00:11:00.080
And so we had to do some
planning about how are we

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:01.289
were going to handle that.

00:11:01.289 --> 00:11:02.830
The other 1/3 of it
was spent talking

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:04.830
about our build infrastructure
and ways in which

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:07.510
we can improve and make our
CI and build infrastructure

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:08.580
more reliable.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:10.180
And then 1/3 of it
was spent talking

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:11.721
about a document
we're drafting right

00:11:11.721 --> 00:11:16.110
now on higher-level expectations
for leaders in the project.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.920
And so, all those
things are things

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.620
that are under the purview
of the TSC currently.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:22.900
Although, I'd really like
to see us move the TSC

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:26.530
towards more forward-facing
proactive initiatives--

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:28.390
finding things that
need to be done,

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:31.300
creating initiatives around
it, finding champions

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.880
and stakeholders to lead
those initiatives, rather

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:37.570
than the day-to-day
handling of code conflict,

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:42.021
and finding more ways to empower
our collaborators to handle

00:11:42.021 --> 00:11:42.520
that.

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.186
One of the challenges
that we have right

00:11:44.186 --> 00:11:47.587
now is that, as far as career
growth in the project goes,

00:11:47.587 --> 00:11:48.670
you become a collaborator.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.128
And then your next
thing you can do

00:11:50.128 --> 00:11:51.520
is be on the steering committee.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:55.275
And not everyone really
cares about governance stuff.

00:11:55.275 --> 00:11:57.400
I almost consider it similar
to, like, a management

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.640
track and individual contributor
track in a corporation.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.590
And so, right now,
what we're trying to do

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:06.010
is figure out, what are
more individual contributor

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.200
tracks that we can be making
for our collaborators,

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.979
so they can have career
growth and project growth?

00:12:10.979 --> 00:12:12.520
Have things that
they can report back

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:14.830
to their organizations about
things that they're doing

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.899
and why the money being
invested makes sense?

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:18.940
And actually, we've been
talking to Sarah Novotny

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.670
and taking some notes from how
the Kubernetes project does it.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:23.839
And we may do some
iteration based on that.

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:25.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
you mentioned something

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.870
that sounded almost
dangerous to me,

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.870
especially coming from
the Go community, which

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.810
is you mentioned
that, if someone sends

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:38.950
a change to the language and it
has not been rejected during 48

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:41.180
hours, and it passes
all tests and stuff,

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.677
then it just
basically gets merged.

00:12:42.677 --> 00:12:43.510
MYLES BORINS: Mm-hm.

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So can
I just go now and, like--

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:47.210
I don't know-- do
something crazy

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:50.961
and add-- like, I'm going to
add Go routines to Node.js.

00:12:50.961 --> 00:12:51.460
Why not?

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:53.170
And I just send my change.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:56.160
And if I send that on Friday,
maybe, luckily, Saturday

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:59.020
and Sunday, no one will
notice and we'll get merged?

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.080
MYLES BORINS: So three
different things there.

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:03.760
The first being,
there's actually

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.115
an extension to the rule for
weekends that make it 72 hours.

00:13:07.115 --> 00:13:07.990
The second would be--

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:08.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
OK, that's good.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.600
MYLES BORINS: --is you would
actually need collaborators

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:11.740
to sign off on it.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:13.330
So we trust our collaborators.

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:13.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, OK.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:14.390
MYLES BORINS: We
give commitbits.

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:14.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.120
MYLES BORINS: There's about
100 people with commitbits.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.790
We trust those people to
make the right decision.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:21.080
If they don't, then they
have to answer for it.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:23.380
And the third is, like,
our waterfall release

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:26.481
process that we have offers,
as I said-- like, maybe it's

00:13:26.481 --> 00:13:26.980
on Master.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:29.140
But we can revert from
Master pretty quickly.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:30.550
And the last bit is--

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.620
and this is kind of
counter-intuitive--

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:35.620
we don't actually control
the language at all in Node.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.080
We embed V8, which is a
JavaScript virtual machine

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:39.265
from the Chrome browser.

00:13:39.265 --> 00:13:42.540
V8 implements the
ECMA-262 specification

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.180
that's specified by TC-39, which
is the technical committee that

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.930
makes the JavaScript language.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:51.220
So we're actually downstream
from the language itself.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:53.980
So I know I'm being
pedantic, but I just

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:56.050
thought people may be
interested in this.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, that's
an important difference.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.150
MYLES BORINS: So for
example, in theory,

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.840
if you wanted to, you could
go and make C++ changes to V8

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:06.850
to change the way that
strings work to be not

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.420
to the specification.

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:10.630
But no one is going to
sign off on that landing.

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:13.180
And we have pretty strict
process around, like,

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:14.380
what can land on V8.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:16.870
And generally, we
don't land anything

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.852
into the V8 tree that hasn't
been approved upstream.

00:14:19.852 --> 00:14:22.060
MARK MANDEL: So you mentioned
this a couple of times.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:23.600
But let's talk
about it directly.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.680
You talked about how Google
is now a platinum sponsor.

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:28.797
Sarah Novotny is
now on the board--

00:14:28.797 --> 00:14:29.630
MYLES BORINS: Mm-hm.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:30.460
MARK MANDEL: --I think it was.

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:31.642
So what does that entail?

00:14:31.642 --> 00:14:33.100
Like, what is
platinum sponsorship?

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:34.590
You talked a little
bit about it.

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:37.840
And what does it mean, now that
Google is part of this program?

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.930
MYLES BORINS: Yeah, so
if you go to nodejs.org,

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.460
at the top of the page, there's
a link that says Foundation.

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:44.960
And if you click
on Board there, you

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.770
can see a little bit more
information about the board

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:48.954
and who's on there.

00:14:48.954 --> 00:14:51.370
We can notice that I actually
need to send a pole request,

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:53.170
because Sarah Novotny's
name is not on there yet.

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:54.760
And I'll do that
after this call.

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:58.140
But the board of
directors, as far as

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.460
how the Foundation is
concerned and what we get,

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:04.070
we've given a certain amount
of money to the Foundation

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.110
at a platform level
of sponsorship.

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:08.090
That gives us a seat at
the board of directors.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.630
And having a seat at the board
of directors allows us to have,

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:15.800
to a certain extent, a say in
how that money is being used

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:18.170
and how the project
is being supported

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:20.640
and how higher-level
initiatives are being done.

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.390
So the majority of the things
that the board focuses on

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.300
tend to either be in a support
role to the core project

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.780
or in a marketing
role to Node itself.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:30.614
So the Foundation's
responsible for throwing

00:15:30.614 --> 00:15:32.863
Node Interactive, which was
the big conference that we

00:15:32.863 --> 00:15:33.980
were at last year.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:36.500
And the money that's
in the Foundation

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:39.140
floats the conference
being able to happen,

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:42.620
until it gets back the
money from ticket sales.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:45.590
The money in the Foundation
also pays for the salaries

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:47.360
of our executive team.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:48.930
So we have an
executive director.

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:50.960
We have an education
community manager.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.900
We have a number of different
marketing individuals

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.337
that we have on contract from
the Linux Foundation, which

00:15:56.337 --> 00:15:57.170
is worth mentioning.

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.600
The Node Foundation
is actually, like,

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:02.270
a child project of
the Linux Foundation.

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:04.220
I'm definitely not
using the correct terms.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.170
But the Linux
Foundation has a process

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.900
of spinning up foundations under
the Linux Foundation, which

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.990
is how the Node
Foundation was formed.

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:13.190
There is marketing
subcommittees and there's

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:15.050
interest groups
that get spun up,

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.510
depending on different
things that need to be done

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:20.230
and different projects
that are going on.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:22.730
But at the highest
level, the board

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:25.260
is handling mostly
fiduciary duties.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.260
One of the great things
that they signed off on,

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.860
for example, two years ago,
was creating a travel fund

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.770
for collaborators who are
not with member companies

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:36.830
to be able to attend our events
and collaborator summits.

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:39.110
So I think, up until
this point, we've

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:43.190
probably spent almost $50,000
over the last two years

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:46.100
flying out and supporting
various collaborators

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:48.410
from the project attending
our events and collaborators

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.250
summits.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:52.010
And actually, any
collaborator or even member--

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.220
it's not always clear.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.740
But we try to let people know.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:57.680
Anyone who's a member of the
Foundation, which costs $50,

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:00.680
is eligible to apply
for a travel stipend.

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:03.159
Generally, these all have to
be approved by the Technical

00:17:03.159 --> 00:17:03.950
Steering Committee.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:06.170
And they're not going to
approve individuals who

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:08.000
are not actively participating.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.089
But if you're even just mildly
participating in the project

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:14.329
and you pay $50 or if you even
just get committed and get

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:16.790
on a working group, you get
that membership for free,

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:18.530
you can get free trips
around the world,

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:22.700
on the Foundation's dime, to
go and collaborate with people.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:24.839
And this is a really great
service that we offer.

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:26.270
We have students.

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:28.400
And we have education faculty.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:31.520
We have people working for small
companies, people who normally

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:34.160
wouldn't necessarily get
these opportunities be

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:37.770
able to come out and still be
a large part of the project.

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:38.520
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:42.140
And you mentioned that Sarah
Novotny has joined you.

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.350
Many people probably
know her from her work.

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:45.460
She's down in Kubernetes.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.090
You mentioned, she's brought
some of her experience

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:49.610
about managing open-source
projects from Kubernetes

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:51.009
over to the Nerd Foundation.

00:17:51.009 --> 00:17:52.550
Are there any
particular lessons or--

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:52.820
MYLES BORINS: Yeah.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:54.903
MARK MANDEL: --or interesting
stories out of that?

00:17:54.903 --> 00:17:56.990
MYLES BORINS: So it's
actually like a fun

00:17:56.990 --> 00:18:01.220
kind of "dragon eating its
own tail" kind of thing.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:03.980
When the Kubernetes Foundation
was starting to put together

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.920
their governance model
was right around the time

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:07.820
that I joined Google.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.620
And they were really
interested in how

00:18:09.620 --> 00:18:11.870
Node had done things
and things that we

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:14.120
thought worked and things
that we thought didn't work.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:16.970
And I ended up having
a call with them.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:18.887
I was one of many people
that they ended up

00:18:18.887 --> 00:18:21.470
interviewing in the process of
coming up with their governance

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:22.160
model.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:24.410
And then, now that they've
solidified their governance

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:26.275
model and are moving
forward with it,

00:18:26.275 --> 00:18:27.650
we then went back
and interviewed

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:30.224
them to find out, like,
hey, what did you do?

00:18:30.224 --> 00:18:31.640
And one of the
biggest differences

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.109
that they have
which, as Googlers,

00:18:34.109 --> 00:18:36.150
you're going to recognize
some of these patterns,

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.350
they don't actually have
the idea of a committer.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:40.820
They have reviewers.

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.110
They have acceptors.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:43.070
They have owners.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:44.540
And they have maintainers.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.340
The maintainers,
there's only a handful.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.006
And those are the
only ones who actually

00:18:48.006 --> 00:18:49.970
have ownership over the repo.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.430
But they actually
have a Commit QBot.

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:56.060
And so reviewers can
go and review any code.

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.370
They can plus 1
and minus 1 things.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:18:59.960
But if an accepter
accepts something,

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.430
they can totally accept
it even if a reviewer

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:03.850
has minused 1 something.

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:07.180
An owner has veto
rights over all of that.

00:19:07.180 --> 00:19:10.310
I also could be totally
not doing justice

00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:11.450
to how that actually works.

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:14.584
This is what I've learned
in the last five days.

00:19:14.584 --> 00:19:16.250
But from that, what
we're trying to look

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:20.360
at in Node is, what kind of
level-ups for our committers

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:21.200
could we offer?

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:23.199
And ones that we're
thinking about, for example,

00:19:23.199 --> 00:19:24.620
would be, like, security.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.200
A lot of people want to get
more involved in the security

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:28.590
work for the project.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:30.380
So we're looking--
and this is something

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.030
we would talk to the
Foundation about.

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:33.980
We would go and talk to
the board of directors

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:37.490
and say, hey, can we find money
to create a security training

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.410
and onboarding for committers?

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:42.230
And then, when we create
that program, which will then

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:44.810
probably also have to go
to legal for due diligence,

00:19:44.810 --> 00:19:46.490
we would be able to take
committers who are interested

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:48.740
and put them through this
onboarding, which would then

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:51.860
have them trained for
being involved in handling

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:53.872
embargoed security information.

00:19:53.872 --> 00:19:56.330
And we're looking at changing
our embargo policy right now,

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:58.220
so that we can
bring in more people

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.300
to review stuff and have a
much smaller team of people who

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:04.480
are actually handling security
vulnerabilities as they come

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:05.320
in.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:08.480
We're talking about spinning
up a mentorship program where,

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:11.410
again, if you go through a
certain training or a number

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.772
of different things, you
can get a mentorship bit,

00:20:13.772 --> 00:20:15.730
in the same way that you
would get a commitbit.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:17.896
And now, you can be responsible
for mentoring people

00:20:17.896 --> 00:20:18.676
in the project.

00:20:18.676 --> 00:20:20.050
So we're trying
to find a handful

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:22.180
of different
initiatives like this,

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:24.670
even potentially
explicitly dividing

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:27.910
the difference between
reviewers and landers

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:30.700
of code, which is kind
of straight-lifted

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:33.189
from the Kubernetes technical
governance structure.

00:20:33.189 --> 00:20:35.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So all
of this sounds very good.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:36.970
But now, I'm going
to ask a question

