00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:19.970
And this includes better
support on App Engine.

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:23.180
We have quite a number
of full-time [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:26.150
who are working on node-related
projects, such a Stackdriver

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:27.770
or Cloud Functions.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:30.680
Node being successful,
Node working,

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:34.000
and Node being
stable and reliable

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:37.460
is part of a major story
for us selling our products

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:40.310
and services that we're
building on top of Node

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:42.530
and building for use of Node.

00:21:42.530 --> 00:21:44.900
I don't have all of the numbers.

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:47.070
And I don't want to say
something that's wrong.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.010
So I'm going to hand-wave
it a little bit.

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:51.540
But I do know that I've seen
numbers that show [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:55.970
on, like, App Engine Flex, Node
is one of the most-often spun

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:57.290
up platforms.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:01.770
On GitHub, JavaScript is
the number one language.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:04.360
Guess what number 10 is?

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:05.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Pascal.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:06.482
MYLES BORINS: Type Script.

00:22:06.482 --> 00:22:07.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I actually thought

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:08.970
you were going to say Go.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:09.080
MARK MANDEL: Oh yeah?

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:09.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
I was like, that's--

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:10.040
blah.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:12.456
MYLES BORINS: No, I think Go
is, like, three or something.

00:22:12.456 --> 00:22:13.970
And it's growing real fast.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:16.790
No, but it's
sandwiching the top 10.

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:19.940
And even just a language
that compiles to JavaScript

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.890
is one of the fastest-growing
platforms that's out there.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:24.620
I think that there
is a certain form

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:27.560
of empathy in the code itself.

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:29.330
And some of the
new patterns that

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:31.670
are coming out in the
language, such as async/await,

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:35.000
are making it even more
approachable for developers.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:37.580
And it's just really
amazing how quickly

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:40.700
you can get up and get running
and get prototypes going.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.940
And yeah, maybe it will never
outperform, like, really,

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.220
really, really,
well-written Go routines.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.000
But I think, for
a lot of people,

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.810
especially when you can scale
horizontally, it's good enough.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.840
And they care more
about developer velocity

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:55.121
than engineering purity.

00:22:55.121 --> 00:22:57.620
And that's not to say that you
can't engineer really, really

00:22:57.620 --> 00:22:59.300
cool stuff with Node too.

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.580
But just, more in the sense
that, for a lot of teams,

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.830
the velocity of
Node is a huge sell.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:05.657
And Google has
recognized that and is

00:23:05.657 --> 00:23:06.740
investing in the platform.

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:10.280
So having me, like,
right in there,

00:23:10.280 --> 00:23:11.810
I see it as hedging the bet.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:15.880
It's like, they need to
invest on Node being healthy

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:18.860
and Node being successful, or
all the other money is kind of

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:19.861
flushed down the toilet.

00:23:19.861 --> 00:23:21.609
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
a very good point.

00:23:21.609 --> 00:23:23.600
MYLES BORINS: Do you
