00:23:26.124 --> 00:23:29.600
So you mentioned real quick
that one of the reasons

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:33.320
is we actually invest heavily
in Node, because we also use,

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:34.220
heavily, Node.

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:38.090
And we provide ways to use Node
on our Google Cloud platform.

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:40.340
Is there something that
you could share about,

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:41.405
what is the future?

00:23:41.405 --> 00:23:43.280
Like, it's a typical
question that, normally,

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.290
you say, no, because we
don't share roadmaps.

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:47.450
But is there any roadmap
you'd like to share?

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:48.950
MYLES BORINS: Well,
one thing that's

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:51.170
coming pretty soon that's
exciting is web sockets.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:53.210
For myself, the
first time I ever

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:56.240
used Node was actually building
an art installation, where

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:59.367
I wanted to use a website to
pick between different states.

00:23:59.367 --> 00:24:01.700
And I tried a whole bunch of
different ways of doing it.

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.090
And I kept failing, both
gracefully and miserably.

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:06.950
And eventually, I came
across this tutorial

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:09.440
using Node in Socket I/O.
And I had a prototype up

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:11.510
and running in, like,
10 or 15 minutes.

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:14.090
And to me, that's
the magic of Node.

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:16.580
If you've just written a
little bit of website-y things,

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:18.380
you can actually
get something that

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.300
does what you need it to
do in a fairly short order.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.350
Web sockets are a
really, really great tool

00:24:24.350 --> 00:24:28.130
for quickly prototyping
distributed applications,

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:30.260
chat applications, games--

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:33.390
which I know Mark has a
lot of feelings about.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:36.380
And so this isn't only for Node.

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.630
But I do think that the
eventual support of web

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:41.600
sockets on App Engine and
Google Cloud Platform,

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:43.330
as a whole, outside
of just Kubernetes,

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:46.764
is a huge win for the various
platforms that will get it.

00:24:46.764 --> 00:24:48.680
MARK MANDEL: And what
stuff do you really like

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.442
about GCP and Node right now?

00:24:50.442 --> 00:24:52.400
MYLES BORINS: So about
GCP and Node right now--

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.450
I'm a big fan of
cloud functions.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:56.900
I think it's a really cool
way to slowly break away

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.980
complexity from applications.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.180
One of the examples that I often
use is just, like, a mailer.

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:03.890
So if you're having
someone who signs up

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:06.290
for a website for an account
and then, when they sign up

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:08.690
for the account, you want
to send them a welcome email

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:11.360
or something like that,
these small little tasks

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.700
are easily broken out
into cloud functions,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:18.447
things that don't need to
be extremely performant.

00:25:18.447 --> 00:25:20.780
If they have a cold boot,
it's not the end of the world.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:24.044
If they come 30 seconds
later, it's not a big deal.

00:25:24.044 --> 00:25:26.210
You can, really quickly,
without a lot of oversight,

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:27.830
just break out all
this complexity

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:31.820
from larger-scale
applications and utilize it.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:32.810
That's one place--

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:36.430
Kubernetes Node Docker-- that
stack, I'm so excited about.

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:39.980
You combine that with
multi-cloud and federating over

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:40.710
spaces.

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:42.900
And I think it's really--

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:43.400
oh!

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:44.970
Oh my god, I'm going to say it.

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:47.314
I think it's going to create
a digital transformation.

00:25:47.314 --> 00:25:48.064
MARK MANDEL: Whoa.

00:25:48.064 --> 00:25:49.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Whoa.

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:51.330
MYLES BORINS: Sorry, sorry.

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:54.630
But genuinely, I'm really
excited about that technology

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:55.690
stack.

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:57.570
It's not without its warts.

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:00.000
But I think Google has
first-class support

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:00.700
for Kubernetes.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:03.660
It's really easy to
get started with GKE.

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:06.450
And there isn't too
much of a ritual

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:09.000
that you need to do
to get a Node app up

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:10.470
