00:28:07.678 --> 00:28:08.590
[DRUM ROLL]

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:12.120
How would you do a public
blameless postmortem?

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:12.870
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:14.364
So this is an interesting topic.

00:28:14.364 --> 00:28:15.780
I am definitely
not going to claim

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:17.200
to be an absolute expert on it.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:18.960
And I'm going to start
referring listeners

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:20.430
to some other articles.

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.364
But the whole idea
here being, we've

00:28:22.364 --> 00:28:24.780
talked about doing blameless
postmortems on other episodes

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:26.460
about doing SRE stuff.

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:29.550
But there's a great article that
came out recently from our CRE

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:32.130
team, our Customer
Reliability Engineering team,

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:34.180
about how to do public ones.

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:35.730
So something goes down.

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:36.790
Something bad happens.

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:38.020
There's an outage.

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:40.590
How do you put out a
post-mortem, basically saying--

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:42.670
and why should you put
out a postmortem, saying,

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:44.380
this is what went wrong and
this is what we should do?

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:46.796
So I think there's a lot of
things we can talk about here.

00:28:46.796 --> 00:28:49.380
I think the short version--
and you should definitely read

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:50.837
the article, it's really great--

00:28:50.837 --> 00:28:52.170
is like, why should you do this?

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:55.440
And I think the idea here is
also about customer trust.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:57.420
So what would you actually
put in a postmortem?

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:59.310
So something like,
why did it happen?

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:00.480
So what went wrong?

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:01.770
Could it have been worse?

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:04.480
And also, how can you make sure
that it didn't happen again?

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:06.780
So as a customer, you can
read this and be like, oh, I

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:07.610
see something went wrong.

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:08.320
We're all human.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:09.160
These things happen.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:10.660
But I can see that
you've put things

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.110
in place now the will ensure
that this thing won't happen

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:13.950
again.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:14.632
That aids trust.

00:29:14.632 --> 00:29:15.840
That transparency aids trust.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.010
And I think that's
good for everyone.

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:17.760
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's important to be

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:21.026
able to focus on how to improve
and how to make things better.

00:29:21.026 --> 00:29:22.650
It's also important
to think about what

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:23.790
you don't want to say.

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:25.505
And to drive that
trust, you definitely

00:29:25.505 --> 00:29:27.630
don't want to say the names
of the humans involved.

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:30.060
You don't want to say the
names of the internal systems.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:33.310
You want to avoid
customer-specific information.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:36.060
So focus on what went wrong
and talk about it from a

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:37.890
"how can we make this better?"

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:39.330
Avoid the blame game.

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:40.580
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, absolutely.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.760
And definitely be aware
of your audience as well

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:46.260
and how much information is the
right amount of information.

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.850
And I think there
is definitely leeway

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.266
and some gray area
there, in terms

00:29:49.266 --> 00:29:50.440
of how you want to do that.

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:51.840
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, they
make a great point about,

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:53.673
how much information--
too much information?

00:29:53.673 --> 00:29:58.020
As well as, how much do you
want to make sure is protected?

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.820
You don't want to give
away certain details

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:01.890
about the way your system
runs, like how much traffic you

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:02.380
have on it.

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:04.570
So you want to be a little
careful about that as well.

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:06.300
But make sure people
have enough information

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.720
to understand what went wrong
and how that got approved.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.370
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and they
link to a bunch of prior art

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:12.120
here as well.

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:14.760
Not to say, like-- schadenfreude
or like-- some really good

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.850
examples of this being done
where GitLab accidentally

00:30:17.850 --> 00:30:19.280
deleted it's
PostgreSQL database.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:20.847
Its Cloudflare has some issues.

00:30:20.847 --> 00:30:21.930
Honeycomb had some issues.

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:23.263
We definitely do this a lot too.

00:30:23.263 --> 00:30:24.921
If you look at the
Cloud Status page--

00:30:24.921 --> 00:30:26.670
that will link in the
show notes as well--

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:27.810
whenever we have
an outage, you'll

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:29.350
see a little post-mortem
in there as well.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:31.950
So definitely have a look at how
we do that internally as well.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:33.491
It's definitely an
interesting thing.

00:30:33.491 --> 00:30:35.744
And I think it's good for
customer trust but, also,

00:30:35.744 --> 00:30:38.160
as a learning experience for
everyone else who also builds

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:39.860
especially-large
distribution systems.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:40.380
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

